Excellent, excellent.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.575
OK, well, why don't
we dive right in.

00:06:17.575 --> 00:06:18.950
We were talking
about Kubernetes.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:21.903
We have had Brian Dorsey on to
talk about Kubernetes as well,

00:06:21.903 --> 00:06:26.090
but I'd love to get your take
on it like what is Kubernetes,

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:28.035
what does it mean to
you, especially for you

00:06:28.035 --> 00:06:29.660
the people that
haven't listened to it,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.039
or just want an
alternate perspective.

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:34.080
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah,
when I look at Kubernetes

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.980
or when it first came out, the
way I viewed Kubernetes was

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.150
more of a collection
of ideas that

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.500
have been brewing
from various angles

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:43.930
in the industry in general.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:46.320
So outside of Google,
the perspective

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.330
was everyone should be doing
some form of automation, right?

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.600
Well, there's Puppet,
Schaeffer, Antible,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.260
and while a lot of those tools
got a lot of people very far,

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.330
I would claim that the
abstractions were wrong.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:00.620
We were basically
wrapping automation tools

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.860
around low-level
things that were never

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:06.075
designed to work together,
like SSH and package managers.

00:07:06.075 --> 00:07:08.200
You can script those things,
but there were just so

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:10.059
many extractions we
were dealing with.

00:07:10.059 --> 00:07:11.600
And then when you
look at Kubernetes,

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.690
it looks like over time,
some of the best practices

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.310
that people have been doing on
the outside world, of course,

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:18.893
mixed with a lot of
things that Google

00:07:18.893 --> 00:07:21.050
has been doing internally,
really just streamlines

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:21.810
all of that stuff.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.600
So if you look at
it, Kubernetes to me

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.170
just looks like a
checkpoint in where

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.105
we are with automation today.

00:07:29.105 --> 00:07:31.230
I think will become the
new baseline for how people

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.299
do things going
forward, and I think

00:07:33.299 --> 00:07:35.090
it's going to be a new
set of abstractions.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.760
So I don't see Kubernetes
as the end game.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:39.430
I kind of see it
as the next level

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.150
that people will be
automating against, a new set

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.981
of primitives, new
set of abstractions.

00:07:44.981 --> 00:07:45.730
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.320
All right, so let's maybe even
take it to a higher level.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.170
If I'm a new user, I've
never heard of Kubernetes.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:52.170
I don't know what it is.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.650
I don't know what it does.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.190
How would you give
me the 30,000 foot

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:57.399
view of what that looks like?

00:07:57.399 --> 00:07:59.773
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I think when
you talk about Kubernetes,

00:07:59.773 --> 00:08:01.221
you start with the application.

00:08:01.221 --> 00:08:02.970
So with config management
and other tools,

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.720
you start with the
server, and you kind of

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:05.750
build it up from there.

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.400
I think with
Kubernetes, we assume

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.740
that Kubernetes is installed
and that you have a set of APIs

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.770
for deploying your app.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.455
So once you focus
on your app, then

00:08:13.455 --> 00:08:15.450
we start to push in
barriers like, hey,

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.980
how do you package that app?

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.570
The abstraction we
use is containers,

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.822
so you're going to wrap
your app in a container.

00:08:20.822 --> 00:08:22.655
Once you have it in a
container, then you're

00:08:22.655 --> 00:08:24.071
just going to
express how you need

00:08:24.071 --> 00:08:26.820
it to run, how many
copies does it need to do,

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.960
what zones does
it need to run in,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.300
and some basic high
level networking things.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.976
How do people hit
your application?

00:08:32.976 --> 00:08:34.309
That's the goal with Kubernetes.

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:35.250
Start with the app.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.970
End with the app.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:38.150
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.760
So you seem to be involved
with the Kubernetes

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:43.720
project for quite a while now.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.346
From my history, from
seeing you speak,

00:08:45.346 --> 00:08:47.136
it seems like you've
been doing it forever.

00:08:47.136 --> 00:08:48.970
How did you get involved
with Kubernetes?

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.030
What's the history of that?

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:51.850
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: The
history of Kubernetes

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.266
is a pretty
interesting, actually.

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:55.570
So at the time I was
working at CoreOS.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.380
And at CoreOS, we
were building what

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:00.380
we like to describe as Google's
infrastructure for everyone

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:01.300
else.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.480
And that's a tall order
just because no one

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.530
has Google infrastructure
except for Google.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:10.160
But we did a pretty good job of
delivering the whole OS that's

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.890
focused on containers, and
as we moved up the stack,

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:15.210
we built the tool called fleet.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.690
And fleet used to talk
to etcd, which etcd

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:20.230
was a clone of another
internal Google service.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.404
And fleet was in its infancy
at the time, I would say.

00:09:23.404 --> 00:09:24.820
It was definitely
very high level.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.130
You can get basic things done.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.080
And then one day we're all
watching the Doctor Khan

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:33.090
webcast where Brendan
unveils Kubernetes basically

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.330
to everyone.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:36.170
And at that point
in time, I just

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:39.480
knew that was the way going
forward, that things like Fleet

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:42.090
and all the low-level
docker script-y

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:45.630
tools would be no match for
that way of doing things.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.780
And I think that night
I downloaded Kubernetes

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.590
and no documentation,
and really figured out

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:53.716
how to get it
deployed, and wrote

00:09:53.716 --> 00:09:55.590
one of the first,
probably, outside of Google

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:56.960
blog posts about Kubernetes.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:58.630
It was a full tutorial.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.540
You guys did a PR release, which
is a thing big companies do.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.700
And we did this nice little
single-node Kubernetes

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.040
how-to guide.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.980
And you guys got number
two on Hacker News.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.040
We ended up at number one,
so we were really proud

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:12.720
about that move.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.787
It was kind of a ninja
move from the start-ups.

00:10:14.787 --> 00:10:16.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm curious
about-- you mentioned

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:21.900
that etcd was also a part
of the Google infrastructure

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.150
for everyone else.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.622
What project could
it be internally?

00:10:25.622 --> 00:10:27.080
Could you explain
a little bit more

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.860
about what etcd
really is for people

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.080
that have never heard about it?

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.300
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yes, so etcd
is basically a key value pair

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.710
storage server.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:37.710
A lot of people will
compare to Redis,

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.190
but I guess they were thinking
about it the wrong way.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.620
Ideally, it's modeled
after the Chubby paper,

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:45.030
so as an internal
service Google uses,

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.610
that has stronger
guarantees on consistency.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.110
So a lot of people
do question, hey,

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:51.360
why do I need such
strong consistency

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.270
when most applications don't
really have that requirement?

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:58.306
But etcd provides essentially
two things if you really

00:10:58.306 --> 00:10:58.930
think about it.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.180
It's a place to store
your configurations,

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.970
so ideally, configurations
would be a list of backends

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.650
that a group of web
servers need to know about.

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:08.640
So ideally, you want
all of those web servers

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.440
to agree on what the
backend should be, right?

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.540
Maybe they don't necessary have
the same list at the same time,

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.350
but they should be
guaranteed that if they all

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.490
agree on a single point
in time that they should

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.480
have the same list of backends.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.820
So it's a configuration
store, ideally

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.360
for application configs.

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:30.400
So etcd is not designed to
store gigs and gigs of data.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.816
So I think early on, people were
storing image blobs in etcd,

00:11:33.816 --> 00:11:34.315
and--

00:11:34.315 --> 00:11:35.872
MARK MANDEL: Oh no.

00:11:35.872 --> 00:11:37.330
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yeah, they really

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:38.800
love the high
availability features

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.930
of etcd, which is rooted in
a consensus algorithm called

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:43.800
raft.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:48.310
So raft is really nice because
it automates the membership,

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:50.020
leader election, and failover.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.750
So this is very
nice when you start

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.700
to think in terms
of data stores where

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.860
it's really painful to set
up a cluster of databases

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.690
and then handle adding
and removing nodes.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:02.240
Things like etcd
that rely on raft

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.460
pretty much automate most
of that process for you,

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.735
so of course, everyone wants
to use it for everything.

00:12:06.735 --> 00:12:07.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:10.290
And I don't know,
I might be wrong.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:13.780
But is Kubernetes based on etcd?

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:15.110
Is it using it somewhere?

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:17.193
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah so,
that was one nice thing

00:12:17.193 --> 00:12:19.860
about when Kubernetes came out.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.080
It relied on a couple
of leading projects,

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.450
so it picked docker-fork
container runtime instead

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:26.930
of Google shipping their own.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.710
And it also leveraged etcd,
so all of those principles

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:31.740
we just talked about
were a perfect fit

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:35.200
for writing a cluster
manager like Kubernetes.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.950
And it really gave
people that feel

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:39.760
that they were getting something
really close to what we have

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.860
internally, [INAUDIBLE] and
Chubby, that combination just

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:43.520
goes together.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:46.842
And now we have an open
source combination as well.

00:12:46.842 --> 00:12:48.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.590
So you mentioned
that Kubernetes,

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:52.740
when it was created,
it was built

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:58.740
on top of etcd and Docker, but
you were working on a CoreOS,

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.800
and CoreOS also had
this different format

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:01.900
for containers, rkt?

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.650
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yes,
actually Kubernetes

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:06.740
came out before rkt
was released, actually.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:10.050
So when Kubernetes came out,
it was all built around Docker

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:14.190
and I would say, at that
time, I think a lot of people

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:15.670
were rallying behind Docker.

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:17.760
And it definitely fit the bill.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:21.650
At CoreOS, the drive for
alternative container format

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:25.250
probably started what we
now call the container wars.

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:28.860
But there's this idea that
the container runtime should

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:33.430
be super small and focused,
and that container runtime that

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:35.920
was built at CoreOS is
definitely called rkt.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:38.480
And rkt was designed to
pretty much work well

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.020
with something like Kubernetes.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.570
So rkt doesn't have
a centralized daemon.

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:45.460
We rely on something
like Kubernetes for that.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.890
rkt also has a concept
of pods, so this

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.020
is a new notion
introduced by Kubernetes

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.290
that in order to
compose applications

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:55.159
in the way that would be
easy to schedule and manage,

00:13:55.159 --> 00:13:56.700
you would need to
be able to put more

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:58.392
than one container
in a container

00:13:58.392 --> 00:13:59.850
that you could
schedule atomically.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.474
So the idea is that
many applications--

00:14:01.474 --> 00:14:04.360
I know sometimes I'll end up
working with a node JS app,

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:06.610
and it needs something
like nginx to sit on top.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.360
So pods give you a
kind of an easy way

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:10.710
of putting those two things
together and shipping them

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:11.210
around.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.600
It really gives people
that virtual machine feel.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.690
You can push these
things out the same way

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:19.136
you've been managing VMs in
your infrastructure today.

00:14:19.136 --> 00:14:20.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.718
So yeah, that gives
us a little bit

00:14:21.718 --> 00:14:24.260
of an idea of how
Kubernetes came to be

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:28.950
and what it really is, but
imagine that I'm creating

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:33.300
my start up and I'm considering
using Kubernetes or maybe

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.740
other tools, what could
be the other tools

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:37.880
that I might consider
and why should I

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:40.020
use Kubernetes and
not something else?

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.853
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: This
comes up quite a bit,

00:14:41.853 --> 00:14:44.280
so I think when I usually
talk about Kubernetes--

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:48.550
it's like if you think
Heroku was a good idea,

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:50.090
but it's a little
too restrictive.

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.094
And you want to
walk one step back,

00:14:52.094 --> 00:14:54.010
so maybe you can have a
little bit more choice

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:57.650
on where you host it, what
type of things you can run.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.980
So if I look at Kubernetes,
I'd say it's maybe

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.100
one or two steps below a pass.

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:05.620
So if you're a start up looking
to deploy your application

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:08.440
and you really are not trying
to build out a full ops team

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.610
or deal with a ton
of infrastructure,

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.920
then Kubernetes gives
you the right set of APIs

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:14.650
to say, hey, look I
don't mind packaging

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:17.380
my things in containers, but
I do want some flexibility

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.200
over the runtime.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:20.240
So things like
Heroku would probably

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:22.640
have some restrictions on
what run-times you can run

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.630
or what libraries
you could use, and we

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.710
have the same set of
restrictions on the side of app

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:28.700
engine, right?

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:30.860
So the idea there is
that in order for us

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:33.710
to do all the automatic
behind-the-scenes,

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:35.630
we have to introduce
some constraints,

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.410
and those are shared
multi-tenant platforms.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:40.490
So with Kubernetes,
since you kind of run

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:42.550
that on your own
cluster of machines,

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:45.210
gives you the flexibility
to run it where you want,

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:47.230
even though our competitors
do have the ability

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.210
to run Kubernetes, it's a
design goal of Kubernetes

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.360
that you can run it on
your laptop, your data

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.267
center, or inside the cloud.

00:15:54.267 --> 00:15:56.600
And if you're a startup, that
may be interesting to you.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:58.730
Maybe you get started on
a Mac mini in the office

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.540
and decide to push it
to the cloud later,

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.590
and you have the flexibility
of packaging anything you want.

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:08.050
So if you want to have a bunch
of security vulnerabilities

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:09.950
running on an old
version of Java,

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:11.680
you totally can do
that in Kubernetes.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:15.060
We do not force
you down any path,

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:17.140
but yeah, I think
people like that.

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.700
It's the flexibility.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:19.950
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.774
That makes sense.

00:16:21.774 --> 00:16:23.190
This is a leading
question, but as

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:26.490
someone who has spent much
of the last little while

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.071
setting up clusters
of Kubernetes,

00:16:28.071 --> 00:16:30.570
where is your favorite place
to get started with Kubernetes?

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:31.587
So getting it set off.

00:16:31.587 --> 00:16:33.420
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I
honestly, at this point,

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:36.410
I just tell people that you
need to click the GKE button.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.480
It sounds like we're
coping out here,

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:41.210
but what I'm starting
to understand

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.900
is that a lot of people love
this idea that Kubernetes

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:45.620
is this open source project.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:49.026
It is a rare case that a
cloud provider provides

00:16:49.026 --> 00:16:50.650
the same thing that
they run and charge

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.716
customers for as a free thing
that they can download and run

00:16:53.716 --> 00:16:54.980
on their own gear.

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:56.232
That's pretty amazing.

00:16:56.232 --> 00:16:58.190
So of course, the first
thing people want to do

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.000
is to deploy it themselves.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.790
And it's like, well, you have
to make a lot of decisions here,

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:05.930
so I don't want to talk
too bad about OpenStack,

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:08.159
but I could never get
OpenStack deployed.

00:17:08.159 --> 00:17:10.200
So there are a lot of
moving pieces in OpenStack.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.054
I think Kubernetes is far
easier to deploy than OpenStack

00:17:13.054 --> 00:17:15.720
is, mainly because we don't have
to deal with the physical world

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:16.359
underneath.

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:18.810
We assume we have a node
abstraction in place.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.519
But there's a lot to
managing a cluster,

00:17:21.519 --> 00:17:24.060
and I think a lot of people that
are interested in Kubernetes

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:26.010
want to manage applications.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.800
So this is why I say, hey,
look cut your teeth on GKE,

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:32.070
learn the Kubernetes'
API, and then

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:35.485
have that sit down discussion
where you talk about topology,

00:17:35.485 --> 00:17:36.860
where you want to
run Kubernetes.

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:38.570
Do you want to run it
in your own gear, maybe

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:39.860
a different could provider?

00:17:39.860 --> 00:17:41.443
But I don't think
the right way to get

00:17:41.443 --> 00:17:43.200
started with Kubernetes,
in my opinion,

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.470
if you're interested in
deploying applications,

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:48.330
is to go through all of
the work of deciding how

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.250
you want to deploy Kubernetes.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.410
So with that said, we
do have to kube-up,

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:55.330
so there is a script for those
that do want a single command.

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:56.971
You can use it.

00:17:56.971 --> 00:17:58.470
MARK MANDEL: And
by GKE, I just want

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:00.303
be clear for those
people who are listening,

00:18:00.303 --> 00:18:02.920
that's Google Container Engine.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:05.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And we
use GKE for two reasons.

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:08.260
One is the C was already taken.

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:10.550
GCE is already Compute
Engine, and then

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:12.420
the K also stands
for Kubernetes.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.320
MARK MANDEL: There
you go, so yeah.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
wanted to add on the fact

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:20.200
that you mentioned that you
can run Kubernetes basically

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:24.320
anywhere you want, and for
those that have not seen it,

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:29.170
one of our teammates, Ray Tsang,
created this really cool demo,

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:32.410
collaborating with
someone in Jfokus,

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:34.500
where they were
running Kubernetes

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.970
in a data center made
of five Raspberry Pi's,

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:38.855
all on the table.

00:18:38.855 --> 00:18:39.946
So that's pretty cool.

00:18:39.946 --> 00:18:41.320
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yeah, I mean, I

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.361
think that's the biggest
selling point for people

00:18:43.361 --> 00:18:47.042
with Kubernetes, that we
finally have-- I would probably

00:18:47.042 --> 00:18:49.500
say like the first cloud OS
because when I think about what

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:51.249
could Kubernetes does,
it's more than just

00:18:51.249 --> 00:18:52.730
deploying applications.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.470
It provides that nice
abstraction over storage.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.160
It normalizes the
cloud providers

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.137
where you don't have to deal
with the individual API calls.

00:19:00.137 --> 00:19:01.720
But you get Kubernetes
up and running,

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.790
you can almost target
that going forward.

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:06.600
MARK MANDEL: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.804
I like that a lot.

00:19:08.804 --> 00:19:10.470
It levels the playing
field to a degree.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:14.240
It's like who's going to give
the best Kubernetes experience?

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:16.600
And really then, just the
consumer wins out of everyone.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:17.560
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
But you know what,

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.380
since we're on this podcast,
I think the most thing

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:21.890
that I think when people
start to look at Kubernetes,

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:25.720
I hope that they see this as
an opportunity to pay back

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:27.140
some of that technical debt.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.015
You know what I mean?

00:19:28.015 --> 00:19:30.350
So a lot of people
that have issues

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:32.320
with adopting a platform
like Kubernetes,

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:35.750
like having an
automated scheduler that

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:39.070
can move your application around
at any point in the day by far

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:40.450
is the biggest barrier.

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.130
But it's only a barrier
because a lot of people

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:45.500
have built up so
much technical debt

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:48.520
on assuming that
applications only get

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:50.190
deployed to specific machines.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:52.340
And if that machine
goes down, people

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.060
run around wearing
pagers and all of this,

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:57.580
and I think this is probably
the right time to sit down

00:19:57.580 --> 00:19:59.106
and say, look, there's a limit.

00:19:59.106 --> 00:20:00.480
There are small
amounts of things

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.770
we could do to make our apps
fit really well in this world.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:07.570
And I think a lot of people use
the word legacy as a barrier

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:10.270
to say, hey, we can't use
this new stuff because we

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:11.940
have these legacy apps.

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:14.270
And the truth is, I think
it's really simple for people

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:19.670
to make small changes, either
to log the standard out,

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:22.630
retry database connections
before segfaulting.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:25.430
Things like that you can
easily do in these applications

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:26.960
regardless of when
you wrote them

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:29.001
that will make them a much
better fit for running

00:20:29.001 --> 00:20:30.740
in something like Kubernetes.

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:31.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:33.564
Before we move to
the next topic,

00:20:33.564 --> 00:20:34.980
I wanted to know
a little bit more

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:38.720
about how this logging worked.

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:42.530
You mentioned that rather
than segfaulting or just

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:45.780
like just failing, your
program should log something.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:48.420
Is there some specific library
that people should use,

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:51.230
or it's just like
logging to standard error

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:52.700
and just hope for the best?

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:54.241
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
Yeah, so Kubernetes

00:20:54.241 --> 00:20:56.140
is like-- I guess
Heroku probably

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.070
set the stage for this.

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:01.740
Part of the team at Heroku
wrote the 12 factor manifesto,

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:04.130
and it was this idea
that when you're

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:06.990
dealing with a more
capable platform,

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.270
you don't necessarily need to
write to your own log files,

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:12.730
mainly because
there's no guarantee

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:15.210
that you even have access
to write to the disk.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.300
And it also reduces where
we can run containers

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:19.484
and how we restrict them.

00:21:19.484 --> 00:21:22.150
So the idea with logging is that
if we follow some of the things

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:24.060
that you see in the
12 factor manifesto--

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:27.170
I think it's like 12factor.net--
it talks about logging.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:30.080
And the simplest thing you
can do, and the best practice,

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.580
is just simply log
the standard out.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.340
So pretty much no
special libraries

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:36.530
needed, no syslog libraries.

00:21:36.530 --> 00:21:38.850
You don't need to really
deal with integrating

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.590
with elastic search or
any of those things.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:44.230
All you really need to do
is log the standard out,

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:47.250
and allow the platform
to aggregate those logs

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:49.200
and send them to
a central source,

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:53.380
or provide the structuring
required to query them later.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.490
So that's the nice thing
about this new movement

00:21:55.490 --> 00:22:00.330
around 12 factor style
applications, standard out.

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:01.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.430
So I think that we should
talk a little bit about one

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:06.970
of the reasons why we have you
in the podcast today, which

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:08.510
is not only because
you're awesome

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:12.020
but also because the next
version of Kubernetes--

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:15.510
Kubernetes 1.2, it's going
to be released very soon.

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.640
So could you tell us
a little bit about it?

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:18.534
What is coming on?

00:22:18.534 --> 00:22:20.450
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: So I
think Kubernetes really

00:22:20.450 --> 00:22:23.310
represents what happens
when an open source

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:25.410
project gets popular.

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.122
You start to get a
lot more contributors.

00:22:27.122 --> 00:22:29.330
You start to get people
running it in the production.

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:33.907
And the details that come out
of that is what 1.2 represents.

00:22:33.907 --> 00:22:35.990
So there's been a lot of
work on improving the way

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:38.690
we do deployments, so in
Kubernetes, everything

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:39.875
is modeled with an object.

00:22:39.875 --> 00:22:41.840
So if you want to
have a service,

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.830
you want to deploy
a set of containers,

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:47.970
they all have their own API
object that describes them.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:50.720
So one of the patterns that
we saw a lot of people really

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.300
engaged in with
Kubernetes was things

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:57.620
like rolling updates and this
idea of describing something

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:00.680
that can be scaled easily using
what we call a replication

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:02.140
controller, which
was a babysitter

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:03.680
over a set of containers.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:05.580
So what we did with
that particular pattern

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.300
is we entered this
deployment object,

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:11.530
so deployments become this first
class thing in Kubernetes 1.2

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:15.600
that will allow you to
describe your update policy,

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.419
your scaling requirements,
all in a single object.

00:23:18.419 --> 00:23:19.960
And that single
object will basically

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:22.570
abstract a lot of lower
level things from people

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:24.560
and be much easier to work with.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:29.140
So Kubernetes also
introduces the config map.

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:31.960
So a lot of things we saw a lot
of people doing in Kubernetes

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:35.510
1.1 and 1.0 was using
what we call secrets,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:39.580
so secrets was a way to store
configuration key values

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:42.560
and present them to the
application at runtime.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:44.910
They were designed for
secrets, but since that

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:48.100
was the only mechanism to do
late binding of configuration

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:50.350
and applications, we
introduced this thing called

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:53.380
config map, which
was really designed

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:57.490
to allow the application to be
notified when there's changes.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:23:59.960
So if you use config
maps instead of secrets,

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:04.030
very similar APIs, you can
store not only key value pairs,

00:24:04.030 --> 00:24:06.520
but you can expose them as
environment variables, or files

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:11.180
on disk, and also notify
the actual machine

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:13.520
that the configuration
has changed.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:15.920
So those are the two
big things that I like.

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:17.630
MARK MANDEL: That's awesome!

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:20.280
I haven't been keeping as
up-to-date with Kubernetes

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:21.889
as I should be, and
the fact that you

00:24:21.889 --> 00:24:23.680
can to config maps to
environment variables

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:26.969
was a huge pain point for me,
so I'm super happy to hear that.

00:24:26.969 --> 00:24:28.760
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Oh,
and also the tooling.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:31.380
So some of the small things a
lot of people don't talk about

00:24:31.380 --> 00:24:32.890
is the tooling has
improved greatly,

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.346
so now I'll walk
you through what

00:24:35.346 --> 00:24:36.470
it took to create a secret.

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:39.290
Before you used to
have to base 64 encode

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:41.872
some file into a key
value, but now you

00:24:41.872 --> 00:24:43.830
can actually automate
that on the command line.

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:47.170
So you can run [INAUDIBLE]
create secrets,

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:49.730
name the secret, and
then dash dash from file.

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:51.800
And it would do all
those things for you

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.702
and store them as the
file name automatically,

00:24:54.702 --> 00:24:56.160
and all you have
to do is reference

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:57.900
that file name
when you reference

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:59.370
the secrets in
your pod manifest.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:03.920
So that's just cut down just
on a ton of work for end users.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:05.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:07.497
MARK MANDEL: That
just made me so happy.

00:25:07.497 --> 00:25:09.830
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I see Mark
over here smiling big time,

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:12.469
and I think a lot
of people will, too,

00:25:12.469 --> 00:25:13.885
because of a lot
of these features

00:25:13.885 --> 00:25:17.430
are just straight up
removing friction.

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:19.210
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that's phenomenal.

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:21.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, that is great.

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:24.700
I was a little bit curious
about how is Kubernetes managed,

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.980
meaning that these features
that have been added

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:31.650
are clearly something
that the community wanted,

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:34.450
or least mark wanted.

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:38.330
Is it really
community management?

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:41.630
What do I do if I something
to be added to the community,

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:43.250
to the Kubernetes?

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:44.070
Do I follow bug?

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:45.580
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Yeah, so the
nice thing about Kubernetes

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:47.500
is it's completely
run on GitHub.

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:50.700
So it's one of the rare projects
where the issues and the code

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:53.600
are all managed on
GitHub, so we basically

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:57.110
follow the standard workflow
that most projects hosted

00:25:57.110 --> 00:25:57.920
on GitHub follow.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:00.880
If you want something changed,
you can open an issue,

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:03.120
and we're not very
strict on how much detail

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:04.460
you need to put in that issue.

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:07.560
People on the Kubernetes
community will do a lot of work

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:10.286
reproducing your issue
or giving a response.

00:26:10.286 --> 00:26:11.910
But if you wanted to
add a new feature,

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:13.290
you start with an issue.

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.962
Someone will either
search the other issues

00:26:15.962 --> 00:26:17.920
that are open to see if
that feature is already

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.070
slated to go.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.360
We do keep a public
roadmap of milestones,

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:24.110
so we're already
actively planning 1.3.

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:26.790
I think that some few issues
have already been tagged.

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:28.880
And then we collaborate
with the users.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.300
So if you submitted
something, we do solicit help.

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:33.390
So we do welcome
code contributions.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.210
There are people help
review your code.

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:37.850
There's also lots
of collaboration

00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:41.150
from companies like Waway,
and Red Hat, and CoreOS.

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:43.430
Those three companies are
doing a lot with their users

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.260
to satisfy those requests,
so it's very much

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:47.390
a collaborative project.

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:51.460
You pop over to GitHub,
and we make things happen.

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:54.110
MARK MANDEL: Awesome, that
sounds pretty egalitarian.

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:56.950
So we're slowly running out
of time here a little bit.

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:58.825
Here are a couple of
final questions for you.

00:26:58.825 --> 00:27:01.660
I want to ask, what's the
coolest thing you've seen

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:02.869
somebody build on Kubernetes?

00:27:02.869 --> 00:27:04.326
KELSEY HIGHTOWER:
The coolest thing

00:27:04.326 --> 00:27:06.240
I've seen someone
build on Kubernetes

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:07.810
was probably about a year ago.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.410
I was giving a Kubernetes
workshop in Portland,

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:14.500
and there was a company locally
in town-- Jive Software.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:18.300
And they showed up, and they're
like, let us show you our demo.

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:20.640
And they show me this
dashboard, and the dashboard

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:21.750
was pretty clean.

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:24.620
They were using every single
Kubernetes feature at the time,

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:25.940
including things like secret.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:27.720
So what you could
do on their platform

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:31.730
was you could kick off a
three-tier application,

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:33.660
and when you deployed
the database,

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.700
it would automatically
generate its own secret

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:37.640
and store it in Kubernetes.

00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:40.310
And the web applications
would wait for that secret

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:42.850
to show up and then
use that secret

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:44.590
to connect to the database.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:48.020
So they basically built
their own platform

00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:50.560
that would automate the thing
like creation of data stores

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:53.122
and passwords without
a human intervention.

00:27:53.122 --> 00:27:55.970
And I thought that was really
clever because they did that

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:59.810
with no permission from
the Kubernetes team,

00:27:59.810 --> 00:28:02.940
but the APIs were just awesome
enough for them to pull it off.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:05.050
So it was a nice little web UI.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.410
I figured, man, I wish that
was an open source project.

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:10.512
So that was pretty cool.

00:28:10.512 --> 00:28:12.220
MARK MANDEL: That does
sound pretty cool.

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:13.090
Awesome.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:16.350
And as a final question,
is there anything here

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:18.234
you want to mention
that we haven't

00:28:18.234 --> 00:28:19.400
had a chance to mention yet?

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:22.490
I'm sure there's a lot we
could talk about, but--

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:24.750
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: I
think a lot of people,

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:27.910
when it comes to Kubernetes,
I want people to understand

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:30.157
that it's not a pass.

00:28:30.157 --> 00:28:30.990
It's not a platform.

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:31.640
It's a service.

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:34.040
It's very much a--
I like to think

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:36.325
of it as a scripting in an
environment for developers

00:28:36.325 --> 00:28:38.050
and sysadmins.

00:28:38.050 --> 00:28:40.550
So if you want to have
your perfect platform,

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:42.821
I think Kubernetes is the
perfect place to start.

00:28:42.821 --> 00:28:44.320
You should feel
comfortable building

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:46.140
your own tools,
your own deployment

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.450
workflows, your own schedulers.

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:50.720
Kubernetes is designed to
have multiple schedulers.

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.390
We do ship one out of the box.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:54.270
But you see a lot
of robust schedulers

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.310
coming from companies like
Univa with their grid engine.

00:28:57.310 --> 00:28:59.210
You'll see a lot more
coming down the pipe.

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:02.110
But think of it as an open
platform for the modern age.

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:03.884
Anything you can
think of automating,

00:29:03.884 --> 00:29:05.550
consider doing that
on top of Kubernetes

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:07.496
in our rich set of APIs.

00:29:07.496 --> 00:29:08.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:13.560
Well, thank you so much for
being here with us today.

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:14.545
I learned a lot.

00:29:14.545 --> 00:29:16.940
I'm actually very excited
about the new version.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:20.320
I have used Kubernetes a
little bit but fully not enough

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:24.690
to as excited as Mark
was about config maps.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.920
But I'm sure that lots of
people are going to be excited

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:32.307
and are going to appreciate
your help, so thank you so much.

00:29:32.307 --> 00:29:33.390
KELSEY HIGHTOWER: Awesome.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.857
MARK MANDEL: Thanks
a lot, Kelsey.

00:29:35.857 --> 00:29:37.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
thank you so much,

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:40.910
Kelsey, for an
amazing interview.

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:43.090
I learned personally
a lot, and I

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:46.692
know I'm very excited about
most of the new features coming.

00:29:46.692 --> 00:29:49.150
MARK MANDEL: I totally can't
wait to play with config maps.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:49.900
I'm very excited.

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:52.119
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You are
really excited about it.

00:29:52.119 --> 00:29:54.660
It's a pity we're not recording
this because he was literally

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.384
jumping.

00:29:57.384 --> 00:30:01.610
So, anyway, before we finish, we
have our question of the week.

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:04.350
And I think that you
saw that question first,

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:06.660
so I'm going to let you
explain what the question was.

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:12.020
MARK MANDEL: Sure, so the
user on Reddit is Prebenl.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:13.801
Thank you so much
for providing us

00:30:13.801 --> 00:30:15.050
this question, which is great.

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:16.400
Thank you so much for that.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.066
I think it's actually
quite interesting.

00:30:18.066 --> 00:30:19.500
So the question is, "Hi.

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.800
I'm very excited about
Google Container Engine

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:23.930
and think it would suit
some of my projects well.

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:26.180
One thing stopping us from
moving away from App Engine

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:28.420
is that we depend on the
search or indexing API,

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:30.560
and as far as I can see,
there's no external API

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:31.757
available for that yet.

00:30:31.757 --> 00:30:33.340
I can't find any
information about it,

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:35.298
but are there any plans
to open this service up

00:30:35.298 --> 00:30:37.430
to applications residing
outside of App Engine

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:39.512
like you did with
Cloud Datastore?"

00:30:39.512 --> 00:30:42.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, that's
actually a very good question.

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:45.130
Unfortunately, one
of the things that--

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:47.890
the first part of the answer
is going to be we cannot really

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:54.080
discuss roadmaps, so we cannot
say if it is coming or not.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:58.810
But regardless of that
API being available or not

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.562
in the future,
outside of a benching,

00:31:01.562 --> 00:31:03.520
something that could be
interesting to consider

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:07.270
is, well, why do you really
need to move from app engine

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:10.420
to compute engine-- sorry,
to Container Engine.

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:12.280
And do you really need to?

00:31:12.280 --> 00:31:16.120
And if you need to,
it's not a 100% thing

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:18.270
that you need to
move completely.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:19.811
So what do you think?

00:31:19.811 --> 00:31:22.060
MARK MANDEL: So yeah, we
were talking about this a bit

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:25.320
before, and I think it's
a completely valid point.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:26.790
You don't have to
lift and shift,

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:28.875
which seems to be a very
particular term that

00:31:28.875 --> 00:31:30.250
comes up a lot
now, but you don't

00:31:30.250 --> 00:31:31.870
have to take your whole thing.

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:33.950
There's a couple of ways
you can go about this.

00:31:33.950 --> 00:31:35.670
For one instance,
if you needed to,

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:38.320
you could just expose the parts
that are using your search

00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:41.150
API, through app engine as
a rest endpoint or something

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:43.190
similar and access it that way.

00:31:43.190 --> 00:31:45.770
And then if you needed to access
that from Container Engine,

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.645
for example, that would
be pretty easy to do.

00:31:47.645 --> 00:31:50.410
It's all inside Google's
network, super fast.

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:51.680
You could totally do that.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:53.610
But if you don't want
to or don't really

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.710
need to move your
application off app engine,

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:57.840
it's working well,
there's no real reason to.

00:31:57.840 --> 00:31:59.970
But you need, say, maybe
the power of flexibility

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:02.564
of Container Engine for
something specific, again,

00:32:02.564 --> 00:32:04.230
you could just move
that particular part

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:05.680
to Container
Engine, maybe expose

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:07.179
that as a REST
endpoint or something

00:32:07.179 --> 00:32:08.790
similar, leverage it that way.

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:10.414
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, I totally agree.

00:32:10.414 --> 00:32:12.060
And actually, my
first reaction, if I

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:13.730
had something that
is already running

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.470
a mere successful on
app engine, and it

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.460
had something that
for some reason,

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:21.770
like I want to use some
specific library like FFmpeg

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.100
or something that will
not run on app engine,

00:32:24.100 --> 00:32:28.670
my first move could be
consider managed VMs.

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:31.560
So if that's a
reason why you think

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:34.110
about moving to
Kubernetes, probably I

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:36.460
could consider
managed VMs first just

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:39.730
because it's going to be
really easy for you to migrate.

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:42.460
If you actually want to move to
Kubernetes for other reasons--

00:32:42.460 --> 00:32:46.690
like because it's really cool,
which it is-- what I could

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:49.860
consider is exactly
what Mark said,

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:53.880
what depends really
on the search

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.320
API, the search or
indexing API, could

00:32:57.320 --> 00:32:59.930
be kept as a simple rest API.

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:02.350
So something really
simple that you

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:04.620
have just running
there and it just

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:09.320
being accessed as an external
service from your Kubernetes

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:10.340
cluster.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:15.350
So I think that's actually
a pretty good idea.

00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:19.380
We are not like a container
engine and compute engine--

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:22.400
yeah, Container Engine, compute
engine, app engine, managed VM,

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:23.110
and so on.

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:25.380
We talk about it during
one of the first episodes,

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:27.470
about how it's a continuum.

00:33:27.470 --> 00:33:29.340
And it's not only
about the fact that you

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:34.670
have to be in one of those
ranges of how much freedom

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:38.175
or how much management you
want to have, but it's also you

00:33:38.175 --> 00:33:40.860
can just pick and choose for
every single one of the parts

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.277
of your system and just spread
it all over that continuum.

00:33:43.277 --> 00:33:44.859
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
it is about finding

00:33:44.859 --> 00:33:47.050
the right tool for the
right job and finding

00:33:47.050 --> 00:33:50.831
